In Java 15 (preview) → Java 17 (standard), the concept of sealed classes was introduced.

🔹 What is a Sealed Class?
A sealed class lets you restrict which classes can extend or implement it.

Normally, any class can extend another class (if not final).

With sealed, you explicitly list the allowed subclasses.

This gives you controlled inheritance.

🔹 Syntax
java
Copy
Edit
public sealed class Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Square {
    // common fields & methods
}

final class Circle extends Shape {
    // Only final - cannot be extended further
}

final class Rectangle extends Shape {
    // Only final - cannot be extended further
}

non-sealed class Square extends Shape {
    // non-sealed - can be extended by others
}


🔹 Rules
A sealed class must declare its permitted subclasses using permits.

All subclasses must be in the same module (or package if no modules).

Each subclass must declare itself as either:

final → cannot be extended further.

sealed → further restrict inheritance.

non-sealed → open it up again.

🔹 Example with Interfaces
java
Copy
Edit
public sealed interface Vehicle permits Car, Bike {}

final class Car implements Vehicle {}
final class Bike implements Vehicle {}
🔹 Why use Sealed Classes?
✔ Helps enforce domain modeling (only valid subtypes exist).
✔ Makes switch expressions exhaustive (compiler knows all possible subtypes).
✔ Improves code safety and readability.

🔹 Example: Pattern Matching
java
Copy
Edit
static String getShapeType(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c -> "It's a Circle";
        case Rectangle r -> "It's a Rectangle";
        case Square s -> "It's a Square";
    };
}
👉 No default case needed, because compiler knows all Shape types.

⚡ So:

sealed → restricts inheritance.

final → closes inheritance.

non-sealed → re-opens inheritance.